#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069821} :activity/id #uuid "d137e3de-dddb-541d-85b8-80566c42f679" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049620 :topic/id #uuid "05bca830-b640-52a7-acad-fecb81ab227c" :topic/math-academy-id 453} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069980 :tutorial/content "Adding a value to $y = x^2$ will **translate** (shift) the parabola up or down the $y$-axis. For instance, adding $\\color{blue}2$ to $y = x^2$ will move each point on the parabola $2$ units up.\n\n![](images/54/54d1dec8f7f4af1345c7f6814e0847a6a3543a5d09800c260f05f285ea3b1112.png)\n\nOn the other hand, subtracting $\\color{red}3$ will move each point of the parabola $3$ units down.\n\n![](images/c2/c245072fcd86c4ba6617cf1c4bde76ef8aa0a9edfc3b740ce8b0270fec711bb6.png)\n\nIn general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny=x^2+k\n$$\n\nlooks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$ but translated $k$ units up. The vertex of\n\n$$\ny=x^2+k\n$$\n\nis located at $(0,k)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "82fdbf6d-9723-58e6-ba17-37a663d4fe1c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4230 :tutorial/title "Vertical Translations of a Parabola"} :step/id #uuid "ce6687a3-902e-52e3-8470-8f99683a6a40" :step/math-academy-id 25667 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069894}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069821 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069949 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to plot the curve $y=x^2$. We start by making a table of values.\n\n| **$x$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y=x^2$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nWe can now plot the graph using the $(x,y)$ values in our table.\n\n![](images/57/576148f61e683e58c508b212221c103d9ef9914f549afabfec92a04ec778a268.png)\n\nThis curve's shape is known as a **parabola**, and the **minimum point** at $(0,0)$ is known as the **vertex** of the parabola." :tutorial/id #uuid "98e8af90-38c4-57f6-a494-cc5214d10c9b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1633 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0b9be9bb-e4b2-59d9-9072-421ffe40dccb" :step/math-academy-id 4515 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069712}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069822 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068865 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068503 :question/id #uuid "9c2447fb-d21a-553f-bc38-243ef63696ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-1445"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36e2e343-4403-51b2-965f-168bbe606112" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069293}] :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Combination of Horizontal and Vertical Translations"} :step/id #uuid "16f0fc68-7b8e-5e99-a4d6-be490c7fb9ec" :step/math-academy-id 4522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069894 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068913 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068525 :question/id #uuid "8106f0ee-a91b-5535-bc05-fe874dc661f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-1793"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55cd6345-30ba-5ecf-8123-8e8d624e794f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050790}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069292}] :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Vertical Translation of a Parabola"} :step/id #uuid "5c2c704c-b908-5a31-bcb0-a757c6f3697d" :step/math-academy-id 6158 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069897}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069895 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068914 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068526 :question/id #uuid "a301e57f-0f6f-5078-a2fb-7c39fef91f3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1794"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac1b2aa5-e238-55c5-a515-7b688bc5a960" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069291}] :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Horizontal Translation of a Parabola"} :step/id #uuid "aa879443-6ff0-53c7-88ac-da0db59d17a6" :step/math-academy-id 6159 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069896}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069896 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069952 :tutorial/content "Horizontal and vertical translations can be combined. It does not matter which we do first.\n\nIn general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny=(x-h)^2+k\n$$\n\nis a translation of the standard parabola $y=x^2$ by\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n- $k$ units up.\n\nThe vertex of\n\n$$\ny=(x-h)^2+k\n$$\n\nis located at $(h,k)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "b17f43fa-0d9e-5d24-b900-72a6dececdad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1853 :tutorial/title "Combinations of Horizontal and Vertical Translations"} :step/id #uuid "7b713819-4a19-57c4-9270-3799e69d5f61" :step/math-academy-id 6160 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069822}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069897 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069953 :tutorial/content "The graph of $y=(x-3)^2$ looks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$, but shifted $3$ units to the *right* along the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/a8/a88222e0210fcd33cf3147044a14e66ce491a4ae5660640c6c1856ef8dc2025d.png)\n\nSimilarly, the graph of\n\n$$\ny=(x+2)^2\n$$\n\nlooks like the standard $y=x^2$ parabola after it's been shifted $2$ units to the *left*:\n\n![](images/1a/1a28e319cc19e1fdb5a378ed95f5d87f6ecdfbcf1bb6a26b76776b23203ede00.png)\n\nIn general, the graph of $y=(x-h)^2$ looks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$ but translated $h$ units right. The vertex of $y=(x-h)^2$ is located at $(h,0)$.\n\n**Watch Out!** It's easy to confuse the translations.\n\n- For $y=(x-3)^2$, we subtract $3$ from $x$ inside the parentheses, and the parabola moves to the right, *not* to the left (even though it seems like it should be the other way around).\n- Likewise, for $y=(x+2)^2$, we add $2$ to $x$ inside the parentheses, and the parabola moves to the left, *not* to the right." :tutorial/id #uuid "5e424d1b-91e1-5860-b0c9-5ca519476af7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1854 :tutorial/title "Horizontal Translations of a Parabola"} :step/id #uuid "fa4f9a55-e5d6-50e4-a705-d8fcd6cacddb" :step/math-academy-id 6164 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069895}}] :activity/title "Graphing Elementary Quadratic Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068980}]}
